WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Part 4. You will hear a professor of philosophy giving a talk on the ancient philosophy of Stoicism.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.000
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:50.000
You will hear a professor of philosophy giving a talk on the ancient philosophy of Stoicism.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Ancient philosophy is not just about talking or lecturing or even reading long, dense books.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:29.000
In fact, it is something people have used throughout history to solve their problems and to achieve their greatest triumphs.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Specifically, I am referring to Stoicism, which in my opinion is the most practical of all philosophies and therefore the most appealing.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:55.000
Stoicism was founded in ancient Greece by Zeno of Citium in the early 3rd century BC but was practiced by the likes of Epictetus, Cato, Seneca and Marcus Aurelius.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:05.000
Amazingly, we still have access to these ideas despite the fact that the most famous Stoics never wrote anything down for publication.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Cato definitely didn't. Marcus Aurelius never intended his meditations to be anything but personal.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Seneca's letters were, well, letters and Epictetus' thoughts come to us by way of a note-taking student.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Stoic principles were based on the idea that its followers could have an unshakable happiness in this life and the key to achieving this was virtue.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:46.000
The road to virtue, in turn, lay in understanding that destructive emotions like anger and jealousy are under our conscious control.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:52.000
They don't have to control us because we can learn to control them.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:02.000
In the words of Epictetus, external events I cannot control but the choices I make with regard to them I do control.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:11.000
The modern day philosopher and writer Nassim Nicholas Taleb defines a Stoic as someone who has

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:17.000
a different perspective on experiences which most of us would see as wholly negative.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:31.000
A Stoic transforms fear into caution, pain into transformation, mistakes into initiation and desire into undertaking.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:47.000
Using this definition as a model we can see that throughout the centuries Stoicism has been practised in more recent history by kings, presidents, artists, writers and entrepreneurs.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:56.000
The founding fathers of the United States were inspired by the philosophy.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:07.000
George Washington was introduced to Stoicism by his neighbours at age 17 and later put on a play based on the life of Cato to inspire his men.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Thomas Jefferson kept a copy of Seneca beside his bed.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:17.000
Writers and artists have also been inspired by the Stoics.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:31.000
Eugene Delacroix, the renowned French Romantic artist known best for his painting Liberty Leading the People, was an ardent Stoic, referring to it as his consoling religion.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:46.000
The economist Adam Smith's theories on capitalism were significantly influenced by the Stoicism that he studied as a schoolboy under a teacher who had translated Marcus Aurelius' works.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:54.000
Today's political leaders are no different, with many finding their inspiration from the ancient texts.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:07.000
Former US President Bill Clinton re-reads Marcus Aurelius every single year and many have compared former President Obama's calm leadership style to that of Cato.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:21.000
Wen Jiabao, the former Prime Minister of China, claims that Meditations is one of two books he travels with and that he has read it more than 100 times over the course of his life.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:35.000
Stoicism had a profound influence on Albert Ellis, who invented cognitive behaviour therapy, which is used to help people manage their problems by changing the way that they think and behave.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.000
It's most commonly used to treat depression.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:51.000
The idea is that we can take control of our lives by challenging the irrational beliefs that create our faulty thinking, symptoms and behaviours by using logic instead.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.000
Stoicism has also become popular in the world of business.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:11.000
Stoic principles can build the resilience and state of mind required to overcome setbacks because Stoics teach turning obstacles into opportunity, a lesson every business entrepreneur needs to learn.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:24.000
I would argue that studying Stoicism is as relevant today as it was 2,000 years ago, thanks to its brilliant insights into how to lead a good life.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:29.000
At the very root of the thinking, there is a very simple way of living.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.000
Control what you can and accept what you can't.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:40.000
This is not as easy as it sounds and will require considerable practice.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:43.000
It can take a lifetime to master.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:58.000
The Stoics also believed the most important foundation for a good and happy life is not money, fame, power or pleasure, but having a disciplined and principled character.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:03.000
Something which seems to resonate with many people today.

